#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119491 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9befd973-a2d9-567a-8808-d126ecaaae1b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given the Arc Length Parametrization And the Unit Tangent"}] :question/id #uuid "219df52e-8833-58df-8a2f-ee53cfed5640" :question/math-academy-id "e-3135" :question/problem "Consider the curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(s) = -\\dfrac{4}{5}\\sin{s}\\,\\mathbf i+\\dfrac{3}{5} \\sin{s}\\, \\mathbf j + \\cos{s}\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by the arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) = \\| \\mathbf T'(s) \\|\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\,j-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,j-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+0\\,j-k \\\\  & =-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature at the point where $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖=‖-k‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119492 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c9d719f-4936-5994-90eb-c33da57639cc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given a General Parametrization"}] :question/id #uuid "ea963bd4-6c5b-5385-8efc-dcd4de104da0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3140" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin t, \\:2\\cos t \\right\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf T(t)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the parameter $t.$ Since we are given the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ parametrized by the parameter $t$ (not the arc length $s$), we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf \\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\left\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\right\\|}{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119493 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c819c5c-aa18-5887-ace3-dfbd9c5700e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given the Arc Length Parametrization"}] :question/id #uuid "2db0218f-bfb3-552a-8683-2f32f230a3ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-3141" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(s) = \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cos s , \\: -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cos s, \\: \\sin s \\right\\rangle,\n\\quad 0\\leq s \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) = \\| \\mathbf T'(s) \\| = \\| \\mathbf{r}''(s) \\|\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(s)$ and $\\mathbf r''(s)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡s⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡s+\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡s+{sin}^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡s+{sin}^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c9d719f-4936-5994-90eb-c33da57639cc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given a General Parametrization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4a86e4d-9b32-5a1e-905e-27a472c6d799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358400} {:answer/id #uuid "85dd906f-690c-5a0a-844e-23aa85fe37d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6t}{(9{t}^{2}+4)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358401} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c2725f-6378-5866-96c3-b1a685dbfefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{(9{t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358402} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d00f59-5e08-5871-a577-0bb0fd1fe356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358403} {:answer/id #uuid "0c9aa9d8-ec0b-577e-852f-2d238ecdd5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{t(9{t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c9aa9d8-ec0b-577e-852f-2d238ecdd5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{t(9{t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358404} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9be4fc6-b0a6-5ff3-aac6-81243d4b9d87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "411461d6-184e-5c4a-888b-610d1e8adfdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-131230" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point $t>0$ on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨2t,\\,-3{t}^{2}⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{6}{(9{t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}⟨3t,\\,2⟩,\\,t\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{6}{(9{t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{(3t)}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(9{t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{9{t}^{2}+4} \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{(-3{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =t\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6}{9{t}^{2}+4})}{t\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{t(9{t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c819c5c-aa18-5887-ace3-dfbd9c5700e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given the Arc Length Parametrization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a268556-678c-54ba-a33d-9faf0be9a7df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358407} {:answer/id #uuid "cabc72c7-dfd1-583c-8d36-efdb93dd4f1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358408} {:answer/id #uuid "95dd5850-7234-5f16-a88d-6d8c8772882a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358409} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbc3c51-1585-56c8-ad1d-4067b4e715dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358410} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6aa1de-cba2-58a8-beee-d4df003deb77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfbc3c51-1585-56c8-ad1d-4067b4e715dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358410} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d143e93-4052-569a-a682-3bc5ec94b44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9627eb7-f5cb-5971-9464-539378dd4816" :question/math-academy-id "q-131931" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\frac{\\sqrt{5}s}{3}⟩,\\,0\\le s\\le 4\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}s}{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\cdot \\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c819c5c-aa18-5887-ace3-dfbd9c5700e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given the Arc Length Parametrization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e5d7158-3001-5492-badf-5929455b3192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358414} {:answer/id #uuid "b985eddf-a72a-5057-9a0b-2cadf1550c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358415} {:answer/id #uuid "04054456-0d8e-58a6-b3b4-2ccad654979c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358416} {:answer/id #uuid "78a41b1b-b1f7-589d-9ced-09467caa8347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358417} {:answer/id #uuid "9d550b3a-83df-5437-9f82-06df0464b57e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b985eddf-a72a-5057-9a0b-2cadf1550c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358415} :answer-field/id #uuid "06b973f3-1c9f-5fcf-80c0-70a73c4ac8fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04cb0eab-4d51-5c82-ab5e-f82970468540" :question/math-academy-id "q-131938" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡3s,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3s⟩,\\,0\\le s\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3s)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3s,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3s⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3s,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3s⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3s,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3s⟩ \\\\  & =-3⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡3s,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3s⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =|-3|\\cdot \\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡3s+{sin}^{2}⁡3s} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c819c5c-aa18-5887-ace3-dfbd9c5700e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given the Arc Length Parametrization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0677aa1e-88a0-53c6-a935-f51fc753deab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358421} {:answer/id #uuid "87ca4763-4f16-5966-b14a-7bb612ea531f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{s}^{2}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358422} {:answer/id #uuid "633c1b15-7374-5426-a128-9b261097d5a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358423} {:answer/id #uuid "357a53a6-4c35-520b-9fce-e4715a9a1588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358424} {:answer/id #uuid "134d8e18-6b80-5ab9-8da3-c4c3a7c5f4f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0677aa1e-88a0-53c6-a935-f51fc753deab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358421} :answer-field/id #uuid "de461b81-f17f-5a5f-a6bd-e8be08c4b1aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33f2de71-7251-5165-b101-a9ebdce74546" :question/math-academy-id "q-132559" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨s-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡s,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({s}^{2}+1)⟩,\\,s\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({s}^{2}+1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{s}^{2}-1}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{{s}^{2}-1}{{s}^{2}+1}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4s}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\,\\frac{2(1-{s}^{2})}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟨4s,\\,2(1-{s}^{2})⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{(4s)^{2}+{(2(1-{s}^{2}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{16{s}^{2}+4{s}^{4}-8{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4{s}^{4}+8{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4({s}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({s}^{2}+1)}{({s}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9befd973-a2d9-567a-8808-d126ecaaae1b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given the Arc Length Parametrization And the Unit Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4b367c8-8913-599d-b565-b95f92cee7c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358428} {:answer/id #uuid "4a38e0d3-5e2d-5953-969d-1d3f6ab36013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358429} {:answer/id #uuid "77b01aa7-c7eb-50c8-97c6-9d73130df290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358430} {:answer/id #uuid "387c1d81-aad6-5dd3-adc7-f703173a6916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358431} {:answer/id #uuid "a67a6afa-31e0-5aca-9d22-62948b77d967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a67a6afa-31e0-5aca-9d22-62948b77d967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358432} :answer-field/id #uuid "22584bd6-af91-51f7-aa49-0f48c9decdda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40d38b64-215d-5bc3-b1ab-04d8eb937bd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-34278" :question/problem "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=5\\pi $, if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n \n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{4}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3s}{5})\\,j+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3s}{5})\\,k.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=5\\pi $: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4}{5})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3s}{5}))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3s}{5}))\\,k \\\\  & =(0)\\,i-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3s}{5})\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3s}{5})\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(5\\pi ) & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\cdot (5\\pi )}{5})\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\cdot (5\\pi )}{5})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\cdot (-1)\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\cdot (0)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(5\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{′}(5\\pi )‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\sqrt{{(0)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9befd973-a2d9-567a-8808-d126ecaaae1b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given the Arc Length Parametrization And the Unit Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c7c7c19-cc20-5311-8496-6e4be12fcab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358435} {:answer/id #uuid "1b7a55d6-33e5-5ef3-ae92-4d6c390d1a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358436} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4c772e-5863-554a-a013-a132e1651ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358437} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8c0c46-ae06-54cb-a78a-adce0bd7213e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358438} {:answer/id #uuid "8744a32e-2880-5fab-bfdf-59a987b6b8ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac4c772e-5863-554a-a013-a132e1651ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358437} :answer-field/id #uuid "f12e2120-cf52-556c-80a4-1db2c2f3c320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "940d7d99-b860-565f-abd1-cadf164a55f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-64656" :question/problem "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=\\pi $ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=\\pi $:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{3})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,0⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\text{ }\\frac{1}{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c9d719f-4936-5994-90eb-c33da57639cc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given a General Parametrization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "017a4c53-dd08-518d-b3c1-4a67e5747ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358442} {:answer/id #uuid "b627e214-548d-5c30-b76b-605d449f6a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358443} {:answer/id #uuid "63cfad54-7e47-5ea5-b175-49268cbac3e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358444} {:answer/id #uuid "729bd64a-ea77-5491-bbc1-b7ded65b0afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358445} {:answer/id #uuid "59769bf3-e14c-5396-950c-a17a351c4261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b627e214-548d-5c30-b76b-605d449f6a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358443} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddfd5e10-2777-5326-a513-6b9083154974" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "879ff44d-4ae9-53d3-be05-23291145ff73" :question/math-academy-id "q-64661" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+2\\,j-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,k,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t)+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c9d719f-4936-5994-90eb-c33da57639cc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given a General Parametrization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aceb664d-d2eb-572c-8211-67c8c2839a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358449} {:answer/id #uuid "df54df4a-2176-5738-ab88-221896135aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358450} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe837e9-bfd9-5cd2-93a8-719b7ab46590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358451} {:answer/id #uuid "76a9a6f5-238c-52a1-9b9f-af5226b21281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358452} {:answer/id #uuid "61949ca0-bb39-55a5-a7fe-609458aaf6e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76a9a6f5-238c-52a1-9b9f-af5226b21281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358452} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c6861ba-8c63-597c-9aea-1e769b4f36ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c85d7fd-dc8f-5b1c-8a4d-1fe80acb12a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-64666" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2})}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c819c5c-aa18-5887-ace3-dfbd9c5700e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given the Arc Length Parametrization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf7f8141-5d33-5f33-a8dc-a0eee2dd8fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358456} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbb71a1-ea4d-5cac-a185-a83042e6bbb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358457} {:answer/id #uuid "95d96a3f-16b6-5885-9bf3-6dcb2f9e9772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358458} {:answer/id #uuid "e69a0b39-43b3-5498-a6bd-e696497105b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358459} {:answer/id #uuid "48d7d67d-021a-5646-878e-b6d76737adb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95d96a3f-16b6-5885-9bf3-6dcb2f9e9772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358458} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7b2f181-6ba2-5c17-8b94-d909a5baf880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81c43925-af96-58c6-a9d2-93a85250f5ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-80523" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})⟩,\\,0\\le s<10\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{5},\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{5},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =|-\\frac{1}{5}|\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c819c5c-aa18-5887-ace3-dfbd9c5700e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given the Arc Length Parametrization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e22e866-42c3-5db5-bd40-6d83839779dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358463} {:answer/id #uuid "8258e17d-f450-5182-9356-c1596555c733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-2s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358464} {:answer/id #uuid "111253d6-597b-5177-a512-1e6803d1deb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2s(1-2s)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358465} {:answer/id #uuid "51088d10-8f4c-5768-ad9a-3791a3cb40ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358466} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9dfd7b-1276-5189-8c70-e2b9aa65314d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2s(1-2s)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "111253d6-597b-5177-a512-1e6803d1deb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2s(1-2s)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358465} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d5c55f6-a8b6-5b1b-9e9a-564b9225a6cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2bbdf17-a220-5afb-81ff-e40c2d80e604" :question/math-academy-id "q-80545" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s,$ where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨\\frac{1}{3}(1-2s)^{3/2},\\,\\frac{1}{3}(2s)^{3/2}⟩,\\,0<s<\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{3}(1-2s)^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{3}(2s)^{3/2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (1-2s)^{1/2}\\cdot (-2),\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (2s)^{1/2}\\cdot 2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-(1-2s)^{1/2},\\,(2s)^{1/2}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-(1-2s)^{1/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}((2s)^{1/2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1-2s)^{-1/2}\\cdot (-2),\\,\\frac{1}{2}(2s)^{-1/2}\\cdot 2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(1-2s)^{-1/2},\\,(2s)^{-1/2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, the curvature is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{((1-2s)^{-1/2})}^{2}+{((2s)^{-1/2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-2s)^{-1}+(2s)^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{1-2s}+\\frac{1}{2s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2s(1-2s)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9befd973-a2d9-567a-8808-d126ecaaae1b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given the Arc Length Parametrization And the Unit Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31d4dc9d-36da-5aa7-bc16-be4f461b16db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358470} {:answer/id #uuid "76b06026-6465-5f09-ad30-d77e8e12e88d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358471} {:answer/id #uuid "75c555e7-d755-5f23-a7de-e6a7211c1958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358472} {:answer/id #uuid "0fedd8b2-1a51-53d7-a72c-f9c6bd3abf48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358473} {:answer/id #uuid "42d5def5-944b-5ff3-952c-f0c525e3fb1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76b06026-6465-5f09-ad30-d77e8e12e88d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358471} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8bdfc6d-3bd0-5a18-9e9f-e7ee9e5ac5dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86a47b66-de80-5638-9fcc-a1a0a4f55dcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-80607" :question/problem "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=1$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}⟨1,s⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}⟨1,s⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{s}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}},\\,\\frac{1}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-s,1⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1+{1}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}‖⟨-1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c9d719f-4936-5994-90eb-c33da57639cc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given a General Parametrization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "828d02bc-82dc-5a9d-ab0c-0a03aa9cead6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358477} {:answer/id #uuid "40f796a6-9577-53ce-8d7a-623db79978f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358478} {:answer/id #uuid "1c4a2212-b70b-5e2f-8605-65d560570169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358479} {:answer/id #uuid "5a6ba93f-dbd2-54a9-bd32-37ff7dc5ea87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358480} {:answer/id #uuid "e009a08f-cf6f-50c8-9c32-45c28b012bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "828d02bc-82dc-5a9d-ab0c-0a03aa9cead6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358477} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c76a2ed-f3b4-55cb-8dfd-56aa5060dc1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a552a20-b68a-5458-863f-bfcbe95a1e07" :question/math-academy-id "q-80614" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=\\,i-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\,j,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4-{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}}{4-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}} \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{t}{2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4(4-{t}^{2})}+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}+4-{t}^{2}}{4(4-{t}^{2})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}})}{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}})}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9befd973-a2d9-567a-8808-d126ecaaae1b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given the Arc Length Parametrization And the Unit Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c63ddec-a0bf-5108-980f-17759a597dc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358484} {:answer/id #uuid "ee238e59-2d7e-581d-a892-7de91e846aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358485} {:answer/id #uuid "57809b3e-73fe-5eb6-aa45-b3375b57ae61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358486} {:answer/id #uuid "bed8ef54-a7e1-5e04-8e42-81532763479e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358487} {:answer/id #uuid "35b62871-2426-5d76-a966-370a9960dedc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bed8ef54-a7e1-5e04-8e42-81532763479e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358487} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6c2a918-7d29-596f-9f34-3fc95c65c6af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b679e87b-7726-5f56-8563-4104ba1cd1f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83660" :question/problem "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=\\frac{1}{4}$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n \n\n$$\nT(s)=-\\sqrt{1-2s}\\,i+\\sqrt{2s}\\,j.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=\\frac{1}{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\sqrt{1-2s})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{2s})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{-2}{2\\sqrt{1-2s}}\\,i+\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2s}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-2s}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2s}}\\,j \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{1}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-2(\\frac{1}{4})}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2(\\frac{1}{4})}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{1}{4}) & =‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{1}{4})‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9befd973-a2d9-567a-8808-d126ecaaae1b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given the Arc Length Parametrization And the Unit Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6f17cab-a60f-5a34-b63f-bf1da75f6491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358491} {:answer/id #uuid "29d47cde-589d-5437-8d6e-ef37f8f02e46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358492} {:answer/id #uuid "eec64b32-6ab6-5d2b-8327-40021fcf9f26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358493} {:answer/id #uuid "8387ce10-0bc1-5ffe-a591-855b4ae20824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358494} {:answer/id #uuid "b752ef19-fb1c-51d4-a3d8-52736da8ac80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b752ef19-fb1c-51d4-a3d8-52736da8ac80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358495} :answer-field/id #uuid "33aba8fd-9eca-5799-b6cd-e5ccf77a6c34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "671565b5-8123-5c62-806e-29b6e861d216" :question/math-academy-id "q-83661" :question/problem "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=\\pi $ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c9d719f-4936-5994-90eb-c33da57639cc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given a General Parametrization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b072038c-1b78-5369-9604-705bc73eae8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358498} {:answer/id #uuid "b1355134-f573-5898-8050-3988459eef20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358499} {:answer/id #uuid "1e91e928-fc3b-55e1-8c86-5c6ce2dc36a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358500} {:answer/id #uuid "0f09243a-4849-55a2-9778-a1a79b250ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358501} {:answer/id #uuid "7503fe00-cbb9-5015-9fcc-29253f463f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7503fe00-cbb9-5015-9fcc-29253f463f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358502} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a285d2b-c3a3-50ff-b767-87c82d5357d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc02cdb1-2d7d-5481-92b3-bd104f553197" :question/math-academy-id "q-83665" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨1,\\,t⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\frac{t}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}},\\,\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{t}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3}}+\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})} \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
